        {
            title: 'Mr Marvel',
            paras: [
                `On the road outside the village sat a tramp. Perched on a stone by the roadside. His name was Thomas Marvel. He had a paunch and wore a battered silk hat. Such men were common on English roads in those days. They walked from village to village looking for work. When they found some, they did it for a few days and quit. He was a man who could not stay anywhere long. So he was always on the road. A man called Thomas Marvel. He looked about fifty. His hat was old and he wore several layers of clothes. That was the whole of his belongings.`,
                `Marvel was choosing between boots. Two pairs somebody had thrown away, and he was deciding which was better. For Marvel, choosing boots was the great business of that day. The soles of the ones he wore had come clean away. The earth touched the soles of his feet directly. That is why those two pairs were a great matter that day. He had found the boots on the road. But neither pair fitted. One was big and one was small. He had sat a long while over that.`,
                `Then a voice spoke.<br>"You call those boots?"`,
                `Marvel looked round. There was nobody. On the road were only Marvel and two pairs of boots. In the field beyond the hedge there was nobody either. Apart from the wind there was nothing. Marvel doubted his own ears. He looked all round. There was nobody in the fields. Nobody on the road. And yet the voice had come from right beside him.`,
                `"Who is that?"<br>"I am an invisible man."`,
                `Marvel started to laugh and stopped. The laugh died in his throat. Because something took hold of his shoulder. Gripped hard enough to hurt the bone. A very strong hand. When he tried to shake it off, it did not budge. <br>"I need somebody to help me. You will do it."<br>It was not the tone of a request. It was a tone that offered a choice while making it impossible. Marvel understood that. To say you may choose, while making sure you cannot. That was how the man handled people. Say choose, and the other has chosen for himself. Then he cannot blame anyone later.`,
                `"I..."<br>"Refuse if you like. Only, wherever you go, I can follow."<br>Those words were the most frightening. Because they meant he would follow wherever Marvel went. That there was nowhere to run. From an invisible man there is no hiding either.`,
                `Marvel understood what that meant. Marvel was a man who had lived all his life by getting a little of what belonged to others. Not a bad man. Only, he was timid, and could not keep at hard work for long. So he had drifted from place to place. He could not stay in one spot. Set him to work and at first he did well. Then within days he quit.`,
                `And he treasured that silk hat. Some years before, a gentleman had lost it and Marvel had picked it up, and he counted it as good as bought. Because that one thing alone had not been begged from anybody. He held that found and begged were different things. Everyone has one thing like that. So Marvel, too, had a line of his own. Only, that day the line was pushed. What was found, he held, one might keep. Stealing he did not do.`,
                `From that day Marvel became the man's errand runner. He could not say no. The work was carrying clothes and money and books. Marvel put them in a sack and carried it on his back. The invisible man could carry nothing himself. Because things can be seen. If a loaf of bread floats along in mid-air, everyone turns to look. So a carrier was essential. Alone, he could go nowhere. Put on clothes and he could be seen; leave them off and he was cold. Pick up an object and it floated in view. He could not even carry money. So to buy anything, he needed a person.`,
                `It was the same with food. When he ate, the shape of it stayed visible until it was digested. So for hours after eating he could not go among people. Because it looked like a loaf hanging in the air. It stayed so until it was digested. So after eating, he had to hide. Once he learned that, he ate only a little at a time. So he was always hungry.`,
                `He could not wear clothes either. So in winter he had to go naked. Even on snowy days. On cold days he had to press himself against somebody's chimney. Because when the body freezes, it cannot move. Being invisible did not make him any less cold. It made him colder. Because he could not wear clothes. In winter he could not stay outside long. So he had to get into people's houses.`,
                `When it rained, the outline of his body showed. In fog too. Where there was mud on the road, he left footprints. Dogs smelled him and barked. Because a dog does not bark at what it sees. So he had to watch the weather constantly. How inconvenient it is to be invisible, the man knew all of it by then. He had learned it only after getting what he wanted. But he had not yet found a way back. He had never studied how to become visible. Only how to become invisible, for three years. He had given no thought at all to the way back. He had set out looking only at the way there.`,
                `Marvel was afraid even in his sleep. Because he could not see whether anyone was beside him. Even lying down, he could not close his eyes. If he heard breathing, the man was there, and if not, he was not, but if the man held his breath, there was no telling. Every night Marvel thought this: is he watching me now? At that thought, sleep fled. Eyes open or shut, it was the same. That is what it was to be near that man. Marvel could not sleep at night. Because he did not know whether the man was beside him or not.`
            ]
        },
        {
            title: "Marvel's Escape",
            paras: [
                `Marvel was afraid of the man. The man angered easily, and when angry he struck people. Some days he struck for no reason. Marvel could not see the hand, so he could not dodge it. Even after a blow, he could not tell where it had come from. Only when his cheek was struck did he know the man had been standing right beside him. He had to walk always guessing where the man might be. That was the hardest thing for Marvel. He did not know whether the man was beside him or not. So he walked with his nerves always strained. He guessed by the footsteps. But that man knew how to silence his footsteps.`,
                `Among the things Marvel carried, some were especially heavy. Three notebooks. Thick notebooks bound in leather. The covers were worn. The corners were all rounded. They had been handled for years. In them was written everything the man had researched over years. Of all he owned, the most precious. Three notebooks. Three years of research. Without those notebooks, there was no way back either. So to that man they were as good as his life.`,
                `The man told Marvel this:<br>"Those you must never lose. Lose them and I will kill you."`,
                `Marvel knew those were not empty words. Marvel carried the notebooks in his arms. Hugged tight to his chest with both arms. They were heavy. Then one night he secretly opened them. He could understand not one word. Only numbers and symbols. The writing was very small and dense. Many pages were filled to the margins. Numbers and symbols mixed together. Few people could have read it. He had mixed in symbols only he knew. So that others could not read it.`,
                `But there was something harder than the weight. Holding those notebooks, he kept thinking why he was doing this. Carrying what was not even his. Drop them and he died; hold them and they were heavy. Marvel walked with them hugged to his chest. And at night he slept with them under his head. Lose them and it was the end, there and then. So he could not put them down.`,
                `One day, as the two of them were passing through a village, Marvel suddenly began to run. And he burst into the tavern and shouted,<br>"Help me! An invisible man is after me!"`,
                `People laughed. Everyone in the tavern had already read that story in the newspapers. And still they did not believe it until they saw it before their eyes. Because what was in the papers was some other village's affair. People do not readily believe until they see with their own eyes. They had read it in the papers and laughed. And that day it became their own village's affair. Only seeing with their own eyes did they believe. That, too, is how people are made.`,
                `But then the door opened by itself. The tavern went quiet. A man holding a glass froze as he was. Then somebody locked the door. And shut the windows too. They locked the door and shot the bolt. But it was no use.`,
                `What happened next was confused as well. People pulled Marvel inside and barred the door. They braced their backs against it. The tavern door was thick oak. A door that several grown men pushing could not shift. And that door was pushed inward. People braced their feet on the floor. The invisible man broke a window and came in. Glass showered inward. People scattered, shouting. And they swung at anything. Some picked up chairs and swung them. Then a table went over.`,
                `As it happened, one villager there had a gun. A man who had been carrying a gun for days. The villagers had mocked him for it. They laughed and called him a coward. That day they were glad of him. In that tavern was one man with a shotgun. That man fired that day. He was not aiming at anything. He simply fired toward the door.`,
                `He fired toward the sound. And he fired all five barrels. The shots were heard to the far end of the village. One must have grazed him. Somebody saw drops of blood hanging in the air. A red spot hung in mid-air and swayed, they said. Then it fell in drops. Only what fell to the floor could be seen. After that there was no sound. The tavern went quiet again.`,
                `The people of that tavern told of that day like this afterward. They fought without knowing what they fought, they said. They threw chairs and swung cudgels and mostly hit nothing. Only the sound of striking air. And when somebody was hit, they did not know whether it was the man or one of themselves.`,
                `Half of those hurt that day were hurt colliding with one another. Two had split foreheads. They could not even say sorry to each other. Because nobody knew who had struck whom. Fighting an unseen enemy, it mostly comes to that. Not knowing whom you strike, everyone is afraid. And they begin to suspect each other. Was it the man beside me, or the man we cannot see? Then sides form.`,
                `Marvel never let go of those notebooks. And that day he vanished with them.`,
                `The invisible man fled into the fields with a wounded arm. And he went in through the window of some house. That house, as it happened, was Dr Kemp's. A house on the hill. On the highest ground in that district. A house from whose windows you could see the sea. That was why the window stood open that night.`
            ]
        },
        {
            title: 'Dr Kemp',
            paras: [
                `Dr Kemp was a young scientist. He lived alone in a big house on the hill and did research. A house with a study with big windows. From that hill you could look down on the whole village and the sea. At night the village lamps ran in a line below. Toward the sea, ships' lights showed too. When Kemp tired of work, he looked at them. In that house there was only one maid. Kemp was writing a paper for the society just then. He sat late into the night at his desk. He was striving to be admitted to the society. So he, too, wanted to make a name.`,
                `Kemp was a man who had made a name young. He was trying to enter the society, and worked late because of it. He preferred books to meeting people. In that he resembled Griffin. Only, Kemp did not push people away. Where two men who started in the same place parted ways was not a matter of talent. Both had learned the same things at the same college. And one was submitting a paper to the society and the other came in through a broken window. What divided the two, this book does not say in one line. It only scatters it through many passages.`,
                `That night Kemp was working in his study. He had the lamp lit and was writing. He heard a sound downstairs and went down, and the door was open, and there was blood on the stairs. The blood went up, one stair at a time. And on the bedroom door handle too. A fingerprint pressed clean into it. Kemp laid his own hand against the mark. It was a grown man's hand. The mark was on the handle. But the hand could not be seen. Only the mark, and no hand. And the marks went on across the floor.`,
                `Kemp opened the bedroom door. The handle was sticky. The room was dark. The bedclothes were pressed down. As if somebody were sitting on them. The blanket was thrown back to one side. Warmth came from that spot. And yet there was nobody there. There was the impression of somebody lying on the bed. The blanket was pressed down. He touched the spot with his hand. It was warm.`,
                `A voice came from the darkness.<br>"Do not move."<br>"I am the invisible man."<br>"I know. I read of it in the papers."<br>"Do you know me?"<br>"The voice is familiar."<br>"Griffin. We studied together at college."<br>Kemp remembered that name. A name heard for the first time in six years. After college the news of him had stopped. Where he had gone, nobody knew.`,
                `Kemp remembered the name. It was a name not easily forgotten. A man of very fine mind, who mixed with nobody, and who had albinism, so that his skin and hair were white. He had won several prizes at college. In study nobody could keep up with him. But he had not one friend. Nobody had ever seen him with a companion. He always crossed the college yard alone. Kemp had seen it many times. Griffin had no friends at college. Everyone found him hard to approach. He mixed with nobody. Many nights he stayed up in the laboratory.`,
                `"Griffin... is it really you?"<br>"Look at my arm. A bullet grazed it."`,
                `Kemp bound up the arm. First he applied antiseptic. It was a matter of bandaging an arm that could not be seen. When he had done, only the bandage hung in the air. The bandage bent and straightened by itself. He had to feel with his hands to find where the arm was. Warm skin under his fingers, and nothing to the eye. Kemp tried not to let go of the hand. Let go, and he would have to find it again. The hand was warm. A human hand. It had nails and knuckles. Only, it could not be seen.`,
                `Kemp brought food, and gave him clothes and a dressing gown. Griffin ate the food very fast. When he put on the gown, Griffin's shape appeared. Only then could you tell where he sat. It was like a headless man sitting there. At the ends of the gown's sleeves, the hands vanished. When he picked up food, it floated in the air and then disappeared. Kemp tried not to look. Watching it was hard to bear. So he talked looking out of the window. Watching food float in the air, there was no talking. That was why he turned his head away.`,
                `Kemp said,<br>"I want to hear the whole of your story."<br>"You shall."`,
                `Before listening, Kemp asked one thing.<br>"How have you been all this time?"`,
                `Griffin did not answer the question. For a long while there was no sound at all. Then he said this:<br>"Kemp, in six years I have not properly talked with a human being."<br>To that Kemp could make no answer. The words six years stayed with him a long time. Griffin said them as if they were nothing. But to Kemp they sounded heaviest of all.`,
                `Those words troubled Kemp. Six years kept nagging at him. But at that moment he did not yet fully know what they meant. What becomes of a person who does not speak with people for six years, Kemp learns that night. And before the night is over, he makes up his mind. What Kemp decides that night is the knot of this story. And whether it was right, nobody answers. Wells, too, did not write down which way it was. He left it to the reader.`
            ]
        },
        {
            title: "Griffin's Story",
            paras: [
                `I had left college and was teaching at a small provincial school. The school was in the country, and there was nothing you could call a laboratory. Teaching was dreadful. The students understood nothing, and the man they called the principal was trying to steal my research. At least, so I believed. Looking back, he may only have been asking. But at the time it did not sound so to me. I was a poor student. So whatever I heard, it sounded like that. I had to earn my own fees. So I had no money for experiments either.`,
                `I did my research alone, at night. By day I taught and by night I did only that. It was research on light. A thing is seen because it throws back light or swallows it. Let the light pass straight through, and the thing cannot be seen. Glass is like that. Glass can be seen because it bends the light a little. Put glass in water and it is hard to see. Because water and glass bend light about the same.`,
                `Glass throws back almost nothing, so it is almost unseen. Look at a windowpane and you can tell. Put glass in water and it looks as if it had vanished. Because water and glass bend light to about the same degree. When I first saw that, I did not sleep all night. I thought this: what if a human body could be made like glass? I had that thought at twenty-five. And I gave three years to it. In that time I did nothing else. I met nobody either.`,
                `Three years is no short time. In that time I hardly left my room. Having no money, I laid hands on my father's. That money was not my father's. It was money entrusted to him by another. My father had no way to repay it. And still he said not one word to me. He sent not one letter. I took that for permission. I wrote to my father several times asking for money. There was no reply. My father, too, was in difficulties then. I learned that only later.`,
                `My father died over that. I went to the funeral, and even then my head was full of the experiment. Even as the coffin was lowered. Even as I heard the earth falling on it. Now that sound is all I remember. There were few people at the funeral. I stood there thinking of other things. Reckoning how far the experiment had got. Looking back, I am ashamed of that.`,
                `In all that time I mixed with nobody. At that school I never once ate with anyone. Because I thought they were all trying to steal my research. Looking back, perhaps they were not. But at the time that is how it looked. They all seemed to be laughing at me. When a man gets that way, the whole world looks so. And there is nowhere to check whether it is true. Because there is nobody to ask.`,
                `If anyone spoke to me then, my first thought was what that person was trying to find out. Stay alone long enough and the whole world looks so. Every question sounds like prying. A smiling face looks like a mocking one. So you stop talking to anyone. And it gets worse. Alone, that thought only grows. Because nobody tells you it is not so.`,
                `Then at last I found the way. It was the winter of the third year. It needed two drugs and a certain apparatus. First I tried it on a cat. It worked. Only, the eyes never disappeared. Two cat's eyes hung in the air. It is because there is something behind the eye that throws back light. That one thing I never managed. If the inside of the eye became invisible, one would go blind. So that had to be left. So in bright light the eyes show a little. Very close up, one might notice.`,
                `What became of that cat afterward, even I do not know. It went out of the window. For days there was crying on the roof. People heard it and were afraid. Nothing to be seen, only a sound, so no wonder. Every night there were sounds from my room. So the landlord tried to put me out. The rent was overdue too. So I had no choice but to go.`,
                `And one night I drank it. It hurt as if my body were burning. For three days I thought I was dying. I bore it. I thought that bearing it was all that was needed. I had lived like that for three years. In that time I had eaten a proper meal only a few days. It all went on the drugs. So my body could hardly be sound.`,
                `But on the morning of the fourth day I looked in the mirror and there was nothing. I was twenty-eight then. I thought then that I had become the strongest man in the world. I watched my body disappear in front of the mirror. I cried out then. The hands went first. Then the arms.`,
                `But from that evening the trouble began. The landlord came upstairs because of the smell. There was knocking at the door. I set fire to the room and left. To destroy the traces of my research. In case somebody took it. So that nobody but me could do it. That too, looking back, was a foolish thing. Because my own things burned with it.`,
                `Other people lived in that house too. That house burned down entirely. I watched the roof fall in from across the street. Other people were living in that house. I did not think of that then. Having set the fire, I did not look back. And until now I have never told of it.`,
                `At that point Kemp asked,<br>"What became of those people?"<br>"I do not know."<br>"You do not know?"<br>"I was in a hurry then."<br>Hearing that answer, Kemp could say nothing. And from then on he began to see the man differently. The pity he had felt divided at that moment. And something else took its place.`,
                `Kemp said nothing. As Griffin told his story, he kept saying how brilliant he was. And how stupid everyone else was. Only when he said that did his voice grow loud. When he told of what had been done to him, his voice was low. It grew loud only in blaming others. Kemp was counting that. It was how a doctor observes a person.`,
                `Listening, Kemp thought this. This man had already vanished from among people before ever he became transparent. All the drug had done was to make that visible. No, to make it invisible. And yet it showed all the more plainly.`
            ]
        },
        {
            title: 'What It Is to Be Unseen',
            paras: [
                `Let me tell you of the first day I went out into the streets. It was January. I walked the streets of London naked. Clothes can be seen, so there was nothing else for it. And within ten minutes I knew. Ten minutes was time enough. In those ten minutes I learned it all. What the three years I had given were worth. It was January when I went out. There was snow on the ground.`,
                `Being unseen was not only a good thing. I had not thought that through beforehand. First, it was cold. Unbearably cold. The snow had melted and the roads were slush. I walked on it barefoot. My feet soon lost all feeling. I could not wear shoes. Because shoes can be seen.`,
                `And people walked into me. Not knowing I was there, they simply walked straight in. When shoulders collided, they apologised to each other. A cart nearly ran me down. A horse's nose brushed past my face. The horse could not see me. But it seemed to smell me, and kept turning its head. Animals do not see with their eyes alone. I learned that then.`,
                `One man trod on me and walked on. Then he looked about, not knowing what he had stumbled on. I bore it without a sound. Make a sound, and people would gather. Even in pain there was nothing to do but bear it. My feet were trodden on many times. Since people could not see me, it could hardly be otherwise.`,
                `My feet froze and were cut. Once I trod on broken glass. But the blood from a foot can be seen. Red footprints appeared on the road. Children saw them and shouted. Everyone in the street looked that way. <br>"Look! Footprints, making themselves!"<br>Because I had walked through mud. The prints appeared one by one.`,
                `The children began to follow me. I ran. A dog chased me, barking. The dog followed to the end. Because a dog does not go by sight. I came to fear dogs most of all. An invisible man, chased by children. The children came shouting after me. They took it for fine sport.`,
                `Then I got under a cart. I pressed myself between the wheels. The horse shied. I lay flat in the mud. And I watched the mud cling to my body and take the shape of a man. A man moulded of mud, standing in the road. Those who saw it screamed. I ran again. When you run, you breathe hard. And that tells everyone where you are.`,
                `I had no water to wash it off. When it snowed, the snow settled on me and showed my outline. When the fog came, droplets settled on me and made the shape of a man. London was a foggy place. And when I ate, the food showed inside me just as it was. So I could not eat where there were people. Days of hunger followed.`,
                `So I had to eat where there was nobody, and hide for hours afterward. Those hours were longer than a day. Even with a full belly I had to hide. That was harder to bear than hunger. After three days without food I crept into a shop. And that night, inside that shop, I thought this. It was a clothing shop. I tried on layer after layer of clothes in there.`,
                `I had become the freest man in the world, and I had become the most imprisoned. I could not wear clothes, could not speak with people, could not even eat as I pleased. Nobody could recognise me. And yet this was the conclusion I came to then. Nobody can see me. So I can do anything.`,
                `That it was not I who had reckoned wrongly, but the world that would not recognise me. Kemp, this is what I resolved then. That to use this power, I must make people afraid of me. Without that, I thought, it was of no use at all. Because all it had brought was hunger and cold and being chased.`,
                `Kemp asked,<br>"Could you not do other things with that power? Helping people, I mean."`,
                `As he said it, Kemp tried to look at Griffin's face. But there was no face to look at. Griffin did not seem to understand the words. To him that question was a strange one. Because nobody had ever asked him that before. What will you achieve, he must have been asked many times. Whom will you help, nobody had asked. Griffin paused at that question. It was a question he had never considered.`,
                `"Help?"<br>"Yes."<br>"Kemp, I starved for three years to make this. Why should I help anyone?"`,
                `Hearing that answer, Kemp began to make up his mind. That he had starved three years to make it was true. Kemp could imagine that hardship. But what came after those words was the trouble. When a man who has suffered begins to demand that others repay his suffering, it is dangerous. Kemp noticed that. The wish to make others pay for what one has endured. That was what decided Kemp that night.`
            ]
        },
        {
            title: 'The Reign of Terror',
            paras: [
                `In a very calm tone, Griffin said this:<br>"Kemp, now I shall begin."<br>"Begin what?"<br>"I shall make this district mine. In the first year I shall pick one man as an example. Then they will all do as they are told."<br>"That is murder."<br>"It is rule."<br>Kemp asked,<br>"How many people do you mean to kill?"<br>"As many as needed."<br>"How many is as many as needed?"`,
                `Griffin did not answer. The room was silent a long while. He had not reckoned it either. He seemed to be thinking of it there for the first time. He had meant to begin without counting. That was the most frightening part. That calm tone frightened Kemp more. Because it was not said in anger. That a man who meant to kill had not counted how many, Kemp learned then for the first time. A chill ran down Kemp's back. Though the room was warm. Kemp laid his hands on his knees and kept still. So as not to show them shaking. Griffin did not see it. An invisible man sees others. But he does not see how others see him.`,
                `Kemp said,<br>"Griffin, you are going wrong."<br>"You help me. You and I together will be enough."<br>It was the first time in six years he had asked anyone to join him. He meant it. In six years he had said such a thing to nobody. So to him those words were a great matter.`,
                `As he listened, Kemp looked toward the door. It was then that Kemp made up his mind. He decided that this man must be stopped. And everything he said after that was a lie. Words meant to keep Griffin there. That night Kemp lied for the first time. And he carried it a long time. He was a man who had never told a lie. But that night he did.`,
                `Kemp spoke kindly to Griffin.<br>"I will think it over. You should rest."<br>Griffin had not slept for three days. So he took the words as they were. A tired man cannot sift what he hears.<br>Kemp had counted on that too.`,
                `Griffin believed it entirely. He even said thank you. A man who has never trusted anyone, once he trusts, trusts like that. He did not know how to doubt. Having trusted nobody for six years, he did not know how to trust either. So he trusted all at once. Trust nobody for a long time and then trust once, and that is what happens. Trusting with discernment is a thing that must be learned too. Trusting takes practice. Go six years without it, and that too is lost.`,
                `Kemp left the room and went to his study. And he wrote a note on a sheet of paper. A note to Colonel Adye, the chief of police. His hand shook, and the writing came out crooked. He wrote that Griffin was in his house. Come now, he wrote. He wrote briefly. There was no time to write at length. That the invisible man was here, and a plea for help. That was all. One note decided everything about that night. A few lines, no more.`,
                `Kemp gave the note to the maid to carry. He sent her quietly out by the back door. He told the maid nothing. Because if she knew, it would show in her face. Griffin had no face of his own, but he could see the faces of others. The only one in that house who could hide his face was he. Kemp had counted on that. He gave the maid the note to carry. And he behaved as if nothing were happening. He ordered tea and went on talking. And he tried not to look at the clock.`,
                `Griffin later called that betrayal. For the man he had trusted for the first time in six years to do it, that was natural enough. The word is not wrong. But Kemp had no other way. Because what Griffin had said he would do was to pick people and kill them. Knowing that, Kemp could not sit still. The only one in that house who could stop it was Kemp. So it was Kemp's to do. Only he had heard that story. So only he knew. Other people did not know what that man meant to do. Only Kemp had heard it.`,
                `Kemp sat a long time in his study that night. He knew he was doing the right thing. And still the strength drained from his hands. And his heart was not at ease. A right deed and an easy heart do not always come together. Kemp thought about that night for a long time afterward. That he had done right, and that his heart was not easy, both remained as they were. Neither one cancelled the other.`,
                `A little later Colonel Adye arrived. And at that moment came the sound of breaking glass downstairs. Griffin had broken it. He had realised the note had gone. He had seen from the window that people were coming. And he understood at once. The police were coming to that house. And the door was locked.`
            ]
        },
        {
            title: 'On the Hill',
            paras: [
                `Griffin fled. And that day this notice was posted in the district:<br>"From today I am the master of this district. The first day is proclaimed Kemp's day."<br>It was posted in several places. The letters were large and crooked. With an unseen hand, the writing came out that way. Because he could not see where he set the pen.`,
                `Griffin was after Kemp. The others he had put off until later. Kemp knew it. Because his name was written on that paper. Paper with his name on it was posted all over the town. People gathered in front of it. And he decided to make use of that. Kemp saw it and understood one thing. Because it had told him when and where the man would come. He had posted that he was coming for Kemp. So Kemp could be the bait.`,
                `Kemp worked out a plan with Colonel Adye. That he himself would be the bait.<br>"He will come after me. So scatter people along the roads, loose the dogs, and spread powdered glass on the road."<br>"Powdered glass?"<br>"He is barefoot. He cannot wear shoes."<br>He proposed spreading broken glass on the road. And loosing the dogs. Dogs know by smell. So being invisible was no help against them.<br>Colonel Adye asked,<br>"Is it right to use such methods?"<br>Kemp said,<br>"That man has posted that he will kill somebody today."<br>"I know."<br>"Then he must be stopped today."<br>And he added this:<br>"Only, if you take him, keep him alive. That man must stand trial."`,
                `The next morning Griffin broke down the door of Kemp's house with an axe and came in. The axe rose and fell by itself in the air. The door came apart in pieces. The maid had already been sent away. Kemp slipped out by the back door and ran down the hill. He ran toward the town. Because he had to get where there were people. Alone, nobody could help him. So he ran for the crowds. From the hill to the town was downhill. So he ran very fast.`,
                `Footsteps came behind him. Nothing to be seen, only footsteps following. The footsteps came closer and closer. Kemp did not look back. Because there would be nothing to see if he did. Only the footsteps could be heard. And they came closer. He could hear breathing behind him too. Kemp ran with that sound in his ears.`,
                `Kemp burst into the town's main street. And he shouted,<br>"Here! Help me!"<br>It was market day. So there were many people. The people at the market all looked that way. And they understood at once what it was.`,
                `At that moment Kemp fell. Something had him by the throat. People came running. Working men ran up with spades and picks. People who had been scattered along the roads, waiting. They had been out since morning. They had stood at every turn of the road Kemp would take. The people surrounded the spot. Several held spades and cudgels. And all of them were afraid. They had surrounded something unseen. So they did not even know where to look.`,
                `What happened next will not be described in detail here. And a little later Kemp shouted,<br>"Stop! Enough!"<br>Kemp tried to stop it. But in that place his words did not carry. People who had been afraid had rushed in all at once. When fear breaks, that is what happens.`,
                `The people drew back. And in that place a man appeared. A young man. His hair and eyebrows were white, and his skin very pale. He looked about thirty. His body was hurt in many places. There, for the first time, the man could be seen. And it was the last time. What had been unseen appeared piece by piece. First the hands showed, and then the face.`,
                `Until then nobody had ever seen Griffin's face. The people saw that face and could say nothing. Because it was simply a young man's face. They had expected to see something terrible, and it was not. They had thought they had caught a monster, and a man lay there. In that place nobody could speak. One man took off his coat and laid it over him. Only then did the others lower their hands. Until then, nobody could. Everyone there fell silent. And one by one they drew back.`,
                `One more story remains after that. Thomas Marvel vanished with the three notebooks. And some years later he opened a small inn. He still wore the silk hat then. Worn as it could be, and he never changed it. Because that one thing alone was his own. Marvel later opened an inn. And he kept the notebooks in that house. Where the money came from, nobody asked. Only, the man did well from then on.`,
                `At night, when the guests had gone and the door was locked, Marvel took out the notebooks, opened them, and looked into them. Inside, numbers and symbols were packed tight. Cramped writing ran on without one empty line. Marvel was a man who could barely read. Numbers he could barely add. Symbols he did not know at all. Even letters he could barely read. And yet he looked into them every night. Hoping that one day he would be able to read them. And in the end he never could.`,
                `So he could not possibly know what was in them. Still, every night Marvel opened them. And he muttered this:<br>"It is all in here."<br>And he closed them and put them away. Then he put out the lamp and slept. Those notebooks never left that inn. So that knowledge was neither lost nor used. It is still there, in a room of some inn.`
            ]
        }
